{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations** in `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refactor`: (1) the `habeas-refactor` skill states in full how to move code, tests and requirements to another file, chapter or outline node; (2) it states in full how to rename requirements; (3) it is the home of these topics, so no other skill states them (the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` requires each topic in exactly one skill).\n\n**Test**: `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]`.\n\n- **Moving requirements to another chapter, and moving files or directories.** `assert_text` on the `## Workflow` section of the `habeas-refactor` `SKILL.md`, extracted by `section`, requires these phrases: \"move the `{req}` directive to its new chapter\" and \"a file or directory: `--map src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`\".\n- **Moving to or between outline nodes.** The same assertion requires \"a node: `--map\".\n- **Renaming requirements.** The same assertion requires \"a definition: `--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check`\". `assert_phrases` also requires \"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\" in the skill.\n- **Home and exclusivity.** `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")` uses `home_markers`. It asserts that the refactor skill contains these five topic markers:\n  - the definition-mapping example for renames;\n  - the retargeting of `{impl}`, `{test}`, `{cite}`, `{fixme}` and `{term}` references to a moved definition;\n  - `habeas refactor --dry-run`;\n  - the node-level `--map 'src/config.rs#fn[load]=...'` example;\n  - the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" explanation.\n  It also asserts that none of the other four skills (files from `skill_files`) contains any of those markers. The reference-retargeting marker covers moving tests and code references.\n- **Companion test.** `bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it` also checks that other skills defer to refactor, for example \"is a habeas-refactor task\" and \"(habeas-refactor)\". It is marked for `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refer`, not for this subject, so I did not rely on it.\n\n**Gaps**: none. The assertions are real: `assert_text` uses `contains` and fails with a message, and `assert_home` loops over every other skill. Exclusivity can only be checked through distinctive marker phrases, which is the normal way to test it. Other phrasings of the same topic are not required by the text. The test would fail if the skill lost a move or rename instruction, or if another skill restated the markers.",
  "references": "a54d07ae817e846f35af61d3ee5e062c81c43bc70e6a4a504a69071debfe727e",
